From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 01:23:34 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] Look up confirmed transactions through the backend; fixes
 from review

blockchain.transaction.get asked only the node: its wallet, then
getrawtransaction, which finds a confirmed transaction only with
-txindex. On a pruned node every confirmed lookup failed, so a wallet
fetching the transactions its history (or a Silent Payments match)
names got errors. It now falls back to the Electrum backend, whose own
index answers for any confirmed transaction; Electrs Pruned and Fulcrum
Pruned fetch a pruned block through btc-rpc-proxy to do it.

From the same review:
- On a v2 chain the backend is asked for [1.4, 1.8] whatever the
  client's minimum, so a client asking for exactly 1.8 does not lock out
  a backend that serves the chain's headers at 1.6.
- The poll task leaves indexing to initialize() until it has finished.
  When the node left IBD, the poll task could index from the configured
  start before initialize() had moved a pruned node's index to its prune
  height, and stop with a spurious FATAL.
- When a new index moves its start up mid-build (the node pruned ahead
  of it), the start-height record and the log say so.
- A block without prevouts on a pruned node stops with FATAL, rather
  than falling back to per-input getrawtransaction, which retried the
  same block every five seconds forever.
- A shutdown during the first index no longer goes on to start the
  backend check and the Electrum server.
- blockchain.transaction.id_from_pos forwards a missing merkle flag as
  false, the protocol's default, not as null, which Electrs refuses.
- A stop restarting cannot fix (a pruned block, an index from another
  chain) exits with 3, so a supervisor can wait for a rebuild instead of
  restarting into the same stop.
